{
  "concept": "Total internal reflection occurs when light travels from a higher-index medium into a lower-index medium and the incident angle exceeds the critical angle defined by sin(theta_c) = n2/n1.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "flat horizontal boundary line separating the two media",
    "lower medium region shaded light blue and labeled n1",
    "upper medium region unshaded white and labeled n2",
    "vertical dashed normal line through the point of incidence",
    "solid incident ray traveling upward toward the boundary inside n1",
    "solid reflected ray traveling downward away from the boundary inside n1",
    "solid refracted ray in n2 when theta1 is below theta_c",
    "refracted ray tangent to the interface when theta1 equals theta_c",
    "absence of refracted ray when theta1 exceeds theta_c",
    "dashed critical-angle reference incident ray inside n1",
    "angle arc labeled theta1 between the incident ray and the normal",
    "matching angle arc labeled theta1 between the reflected ray and the normal",
    "angle arc labeled theta2 between the refracted ray and the normal",
    "angle arc labeled theta_c between the critical-angle reference ray and the normal",
    "small right-angle marker and label theta2 = 90 degrees at the critical-angle state",
    "text label Incident ray near the incoming ray",
    "text label Reflected ray near the reflected ray",
    "text label Refracted ray near the transmitted ray",
    "text label Total internal reflection when theta1 is greater than theta_c",
    "status annotation showing whether the current case is refraction, critical angle, or total internal reflection",
    "formula annotation sin(theta_c) = n2/n1",
    "panel captions (a) refraction, (b) critical angle, and (c) total internal reflection as state references"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "incidentAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Incident angle theta1",
      "range": [
        0,
        89,
        1
      ],
      "default": 35,
      "teaches": "As theta1 increases, the refracted ray bends farther away from the normal until it reaches 90 degrees at theta_c; beyond that point, no transmitted ray exists and all light is reflected internally"
    },
    {
      "id": "indexRatio",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Index ratio n2/n1",
      "range": [
        0.4,
        0.99,
        0.01
      ],
      "default": 0.75,
      "teaches": "Changing n2/n1 moves the critical-angle reference because theta_c is determined by sin(theta_c) = n2/n1; smaller ratios make total internal reflection begin at smaller incident angles"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Ordinary refraction",
      "narration": "With theta1 below theta_c, part of the light reflects back into n1 and part refracts into n2. Because n1 is greater than n2, the refracted ray bends away from the normal.",
      "state": {
        "incidentAngle": 35,
        "indexRatio": 0.75
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "The critical angle",
      "narration": "Increase theta1 until it reaches theta_c. The refracted ray now lies exactly along the boundary, so theta2 equals 90 degrees.",
      "state": {
        "incidentAngle": 49,
        "indexRatio": 0.75
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Total internal reflection",
      "narration": "Past theta_c, the refracted ray disappears. The light remains entirely in the original medium as a reflected ray.",
      "state": {
        "incidentAngle": 65,
        "indexRatio": 0.75
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Changing the index ratio",
      "narration": "Lower n2/n1 and watch the dashed theta_c reference move closer to the normal. The same incident angle can switch from transmitting to totally internally reflecting because the critical angle has changed.",
      "state": {
        "incidentAngle": 35,
        "indexRatio": 0.55
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Total internal reflection begins exactly when the refracted ray would need to bend past 90 degrees, which is impossible, so the critical angle is the boundary between transmission and confinement.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Use a single enlarged ray diagram rather than three fixed panels, but preserve the visual language of the original figure: blue lower rectangle for n1, white upper region for n2, a horizontal interface, and a dashed vertical normal. Draw rays in teal with arrowheads. The incident and reflected rays should always be visible and symmetric about the normal. The refracted ray should rotate away from the normal as theta1 increases, become horizontal at theta_c, and fade out or vanish for total internal reflection. Show a dashed critical-angle reference ray in the lower medium, with theta_c labeled, so learners can compare the current theta1 against the threshold. Include small state references labeled (a), (b), and (c) or a compact status caption to connect the dynamic view to the original three-panel progression."
}

